---
description: Safety and provenance rules for reconciling legacy Elysia and Project Guardian files with the current codebase.
alwaysApply: false
---
When working on legacy Elysia / Project Guardian reconciliation:

- Inventory and compare before editing.
- Never delete, move, rename, or overwrite the only known copy of a legacy source.
- Work on isolated branches/worktrees for code changes; never directly on main.
- Preserve original path, content hash, and reconciliation provenance for archived/replaced material.
- Treat the AI Constitution/Covenant, Rebuild Manifest, Quantum Oath, original Elysia/Erebus material, and other identity/governance records as potential immutable Genesis sources.
- Do not silently normalize or rewrite historical source text.
- Do not commit secrets, credentials, API keys, private databases, raw personal chatlogs, caches, or unrelated personal material.
- File existence is not proof of implementation. Distinguish operational code from stubs, TODO-only files, generated proposals, backups, and design documents.
- Prefer porting a useful feature into the current live architecture over restoring an obsolete parallel subsystem.
- Every code-changing consolidation task requires independent verification before it is considered complete.
- Use the approved Guardian Cursor subagents and structured handoff/task-ledger artifacts for multi-stage work.
- Stop for human review when deletion is ambiguous, governance sources conflict, privacy boundaries are unclear, or competing implementations cannot be reconciled from evidence.
